Ascension 2: Valere B10.

This was miserable.  I ascended my tournament orcish barbarian first
try (although a day after the tournament closed), but it took me 10
tries to get a Barbarian winner this time.

Of the first 9 Valere B's, most died in uninteresting ways, because
they got cornered and ran out of hit points.  Lesson: (1) Watch your
hit points.  (2) Maneuver.  (3) Don't gain levels too fast.

Valere B4 decided to fight a black dragon in the castle without
resistance, but didn't corner it in that little hall.  Result - the
dragon fled across a pit, then breathed Valere to death.  Lesson:  (1)
Don't do that.  (2) Jump in the pit, idiot!

Valere B7 was at the castle without MR, and didn't notice that the
master liches were destroying his armor.  He was then unpleasantly
surprised when a purple worm digested him immediately after eating.
Lesson:  (1) Watch your messages.  (2) Watch out for purple worms.

Valere B9 went on a murder spree in minetown, then tried to convert the
altar.  Big mistake - chaotics can murder without losing telepathy, but
apparently their gods still don't like it.  The newly neutral Valere
was so depressed he let an ant eat him.

Valere B10 made it.  He dropped to minetown to get armor and check for
magic lamps, then went back up and did sokoban in the hope of getting
reflection.  No luck, so he finished the mines, safely, then went about
his business.

An armor shop paid off with a cloak of displacement, which he wore for
the rest of the game.  Sacrificing gave him Stormy, and a ghost file
coughed up Magicbane, so he was in good shape for weapons.  He never
came up with a katana or saber until after he had enchanted a
longsword, so he #twoweaponed the artifact/longsword combo for the rest
of the game.

Ludios was great - Valere finally figured out to stand two squares back
and one up or down from the door to the main chamber, which protected
him from wands (no reflection) and made all the soldiers come in and
stand on the same spot.  A +7 Stormy/+7 longsword made short work of
the soldiers, and a silver dragon by the moat and grey dragon in the
castle each had scales, saving him the planned use of his /UD.  Thanks
to a brief session as a Xorn, Valere ended up with permanent +2 acc/+4
dmg and protection from shape changers.

Then Valere did something mildly stupid - he drank a blessed potion of
invisibility instead of *see invisible*.  I briefly toyed with going to
an "LS/SDSM/[MR combo, but ultimately stuck with
"Reflection/GDSM/[Displacement.

The rest of the game was easy.  In particular, Gehennom went smoothly -
V bought clairvoyance a few times from the Valley priest, and saved up
enough cursed !ofGL to skip all the non-teleport levels on the way
back.  At the planes  V only had 3 scrolls of gold detection, and
accidentially read one while stunned rather than confused, meaning he
really only had 2.  He found the portals in earth and fire by wearing
the Amulet, which was a royal pain.

Finally, on the Astral Plane, V tried lowering Pestilence's max HP by
pitching 8 !FH at him.  The plan was to see if Valere could get
Pestilence to the point where he could reliably kill him on the first
turn and run up his score that way, but Pestilence has a hella saving
throw, so Valere gave up and sacrificed the Amulet.

